================================================================================
## Registry without Lakebase: SQLite backend for regions where Lakebase isn't offered
================================================================================

Context:
Lakebase is not offered in every cloud region yet, and
a Lakebase project must live in the workspace's region. The registry is the
only part of OntoBricks that needs Lakebase: the graph and the Explorer run on
Delta with the Lakehouse backend, and the MCP server goes through the app's
REST API. This adds REGISTRY_BACKEND=sqlite: the Lakebase registry mixins run
unchanged on an embedded SQLite file, which is snapshotted to the registry
UC Volume because a Databricks App's local disk is wiped on restart.
Lakebase stays the default; nothing changes for existing deployments.

Changes:

1. src/back/objects/registry/store/sqlite/ (new subpackage)
   - SQLiteDialect: rewrites the registry's Postgres SQL for SQLite (%s
     placeholders, :: casts, DELETE ... USING, UPDATE/DELETE aliases,
     string_to_array version ordering, jsonb_set / jsonb_build_object,
     make_interval lease checks, array_agg(...)[1], = ANY(%s), ON CONFLICT
     ON CONSTRAINT). Reports which parameters need a list or timestamp
     conversion.
   - SQLiteConnection: the psycopg subset the mixins use (cursor with
     dict rows, fetch*, rowcount, executemany, transaction savepoints).
     The outermost block is one transaction; work is serialised per file and
     nested _connect() calls share the outer connection. Values come back as
     psycopg returns them (jsonb -> dict/list, timestamptz -> aware
     datetime, boolean -> bool). Registers now(), gen_random_uuid(),
     version_key() and the first_by aggregate.
   - SQLiteRegistryStore: subclass of LakebaseRegistryStore that replaces
     the connection, the schema (created when the file is new; Postgres
     self-healing migrations skipped) and the Postgres-only diagnostics.
     No Initialize step: the registry row is seeded on first use.
   - SQLiteSnapshot: restores the file from the Volume at startup, uploads a
     consistent copy (SQLite backup API) a few seconds after writes and at
     exit when dirty. One shared instance per file.
   - schema.sql: SQLite copy of the Lakebase registry schema.
2. src/back/objects/registry/store/factory.py
   RegistryFactory.sqlite(); from_cfg() picks the backend from settings
   (backend_name()); default snapshot path /Volumes/<cat>/<sch>/<vol>/_registry/<schema>.sqlite.
3. src/back/objects/registry/RegistryService.py,
   src/back/objects/registry/collab/EditLockService.py,
   src/back/objects/domain/settings/RegistrySettings.py
   The five hardcoded RegistryFactory.lakebase(...) calls go through from_cfg().
4. src/shared/config/settings.py
   New registry_backend setting (REGISTRY_BACKEND, default lakebase).
5. src/back/core/graphdb/GraphDBFactory.py, src/back/objects/session/DomainSession.py
   default_graph_backend(): new or unset domains default to the Lakehouse
   backend when the registry runs on SQLite.
6. src/shared/fastapi/health.py
   With the SQLite registry the "lakebase" check reports the SQLite registry
   (file, snapshot, readiness) and Lakebase-only checks report "Not used"
   (@_lakebase_only decorator).
7. src/front/routes/home.py, src/front/templates/partials/registry/_registry_configuration.html
   Settings -> Registry Location describes the SQLite registry (file, Volume
   snapshot, limits) instead of the Lakebase connection panel.
8. app.yaml.template, scripts/deploy.config.sh, .env.example, docs/deployment.md
   REGISTRY_BACKEND rendered from APP_REGISTRY_BACKEND (default lakebase);
   deployment guide section "Regions without Lakebase".
9. tests/units/registry/test_sqlite_registry_store.py (new),
   tests/units/registry/test_registry_factory_backend.py (new),
   tests/units/settings/test_v090_settings_registry.py
   51 new tests run every registry mixin end to end on a real SQLite file
   (catalog, permissions, config, schedules, audit, comments, tasks, edit
   locks incl. stale reclaim, build runs and rollups, graph analytics,
   documents), the dialect rules, the snapshot round trip after a lost local
   disk, backend selection and health. Settings tests now patch from_cfg.

Known limits: one app instance only (single writer); a crash can lose the
writes made since the last snapshot (a few seconds).

